---
/**
 * /pricing — ported from the legacy `pricing.html`, structure intact.
 *
 * WHY THIS PAGE IS A PORT AND NOT A REDESIGN. specs/DESIGN-DECISIONS.md §0b
 * forbids "porting a legacy page and calling it designed", and §1 lists pricing
 * among the three routes due a full redesign. The owner overrode both for this
 * page specifically: "For pricing I already told you to keep the same style what
 * we have in live. Refine the messaging and do correction if needed, but design
 * must remain same." So the STRUCTURE is the legacy page's, section for section,
 * and the EXECUTION is this rebuild's: Section.astro head blocks, `surface`
 * cards, tokens only. Nothing here restates the card recipe or a heading size.
 *
 * ── OA-05 IS RESOLVED HERE, FROM THE PLATFORM REPO, NOT GUESSED ──────────────
 *
 * OA-05 blocked this port: pricing said "Unlimited" where the platform was
 * believed to enforce caps of "Starter 5/mo, Pro 25/mo", per a comment in
 * pricing.html:524. Both halves of that comment turned out to be wrong, and the
 * evidence is in two files:
 *
 *   1. crowagent-platform/web/lib/stripe/tier-limits.ts, header comment:
 *      `CROWMARK_TIER_EVAL_LIMITS` counted rows in `crowmark_evaluations`, a
 *      table that DOES NOT EXIST in production. The count came back null, the
 *      guard passed for everyone, and it has since been deleted. There is no
 *      5/25 CrowMark cap and there never was one that ran. The 5 and 25 still in
 *      that file are `CORE_TIER_PROPERTY_LIMITS` — properties, on CrowAgent
 *      Core, a discontinued product. The pricing-page comment attributed one
 *      product's dead limits to another product's live ones.
 *
 *   2. crowagent-platform/web/src/shared/lib/credit-allowances.ts is the single
 *      source of truth for what IS enforced, and tier-limits.ts now reads it:
 *      CrowMark Starter 200, Pro 750, Portfolio 3,000 AI credits per calendar
 *      month. Seats come from billing/seat-limits.ts: 3 / 10 / unlimited.
 *
 * So the honest page is neither "unlimited everywhere" nor "5 and 25":
 *   - Bids are NOT metered. "Unlimited" is true and stays.
 *   - Seats are 3 / 10 / unlimited. Already correct on the legacy page.
 *   - AI credits ARE metered, and the numbers were never published at all. The
 *     legacy page described the credit model in detail and then never said how
 *     many you get. They are published here, on the cards, in the comparison
 *     table and in their own section.
 *   - Portfolio is therefore NOT "bid volume effectively unlimited": it is
 *     3,000 credits a month. That line is corrected.
 *
 * ── OTHER CORRECTIONS, EACH WITH ITS SOURCE ─────────────────────────────────
 *
 *   PPN 002 dates      "Feb 2025" was a bare month on a page that argues for
 *                      precision. Published 13 February 2025, mandatory from
 *                      1 October 2025. Never 24 February, which is the
 *                      Procurement Act commencement date. Matches /sources.
 *   s.52 vs s.71       The legacy page merged them into one "s.52 / s.71 KPI
 *                      check" row. They are two duties (OA-26): s.52 SETS and
 *                      PUBLISHES the indicators before the contract is entered;
 *                      s.71 ASSESSES performance against them and publishes the
 *                      assessment. Two rows, two verbs.
 *   Market scope       The hero and the FAQ lead scoped the product to "UK
 *                      suppliers" and "UK public sector suppliers". Owner
 *                      decision, 2026-08-02: market-neutral, with UK public as
 *                      the proof point. The PPN 002 / Procurement Act capsules
 *                      stay, because they ARE the proof point.
 *   One product        "the Pro tier of your chosen product" implied a product
 *                      switcher that no longer exists. The regulatory strip
 *                      still carried a "Mark:" label from the same era.
 *   Buyer credits      The buyer panel said the AI credit model "works the same
 *                      way". credit-allowances.ts maps every `council_*` tier to
 *                      null, which the file defines as fair use with
 *                      deliberately no published number. Corrected.
 *   Downgrade block    tier-limits.ts blocks a downgrade when this month's credit
 *                      usage already exceeds the target plan. A customer meets
 *                      that, so it is published rather than discovered.
 *   SSO                The FAQ said broader SAML 2.0 SSO was "on our roadmap"
 *                      and the trust list marked it pending. It ships:
 *                      app/api/auth/sso/[org]/{login,acs,metadata} plus a config
 *                      form, SCIM token manager and domain verification. Stated
 *                      as existing, with NO tier attached, because which plan
 *                      includes it is a commercial packaging question I could
 *                      not answer from the repo. Flagged to the owner.
 *   Beta language      BETA-MODE.md §1 forbids beta language on this site. The
 *                      access answers say access is by request, and stop there.
 *                      CORRECTED 2026-08-04, A-54: this said "the TRIAL answers
 *                      say access is by request", which was describing answers
 *                      that in fact promised a 14-day trial — a comment
 *                      asserting the opposite of the file it sits in. Both
 *                      answers are now genuinely access-by-request, so the
 *                      sentence is true for the first time. Directive W8.
 *
 * ── £49 AND NOT £99 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * specs/architecture/DEPLOYMENT-AND-RELEASE.md §3.2 records the 2026-07-30 OG
 * correction that removed a "CrowMark from £99/mo" price as NON-EXISTENT. £49 is
 * the live figure, on pricing.html, in data/crowmark.ts and on /crowmark.
 *
 * ── NOTHING ON THIS PAGE DEPENDS ON JAVASCRIPT TO BE READABLE ───────────────
 *
 * REWRITTEN 2026-08-03. This heading read "NO JAVASCRIPT ANYWHERE ON THIS PAGE"
 * and it is worth recording why that was the wrong rule, because it was very
 * nearly the right one.
 *
 * The legacy page needed three scripts: a tab switcher that `display:none`s the
 * buyer panel, a billing toggle, and a sticky-bar condenser. The objection to
 * the first was correct and still stands: that page shipped
 * `<div id="buyers-p" style="display:none" hidden>` in its HTML, so with
 * JavaScript off an entire priced offering was invisible with no route to it.
 * The conclusion drawn from it was not. Deleting the switcher removed the
 * failure by removing the feature, and left a control that looked like a
 * switcher, was two anchor links, and changed nothing — which is what the owner
 * reported on 2026-08-03.
 *
 * The rule that survives is about the READER, not about the script tag:
 *
 *   - The two audience sections are ALWAYS rendered by the server, visible, with
 *     the tabs as real in-page links. components/ui/TabSwitcher.astro then
 *     upgrades that into a tablist. If its script never runs, the page is
 *     exactly what it was yesterday and every link still resolves. The full
 *     argument, and the reason the switching behaviour is shared rather than
 *     written into this page, is in scripts/tabs.ts.
 *   - The monthly/annual toggle is a real checkbox driving CSS. The default,
 *     unchecked, is the monthly price, which is the higher effective rate and
 *     therefore the honest default. Both figures are in the HTML at all times.
 *     Where CSS cannot reach (a browser without the cascade), the monthly price
 *     shows and nothing is claimed that is not true. Since 2026-08-04 it is also
 *     hidden while the buyer panel is up (O-57), and that hiding IS the one
 *     thing on this page the script does rather than the cascade. It is not a
 *     hole: with JavaScript off both audience panels are showing, so a switch
 *     governing the supplier prices is exactly as relevant as the supplier
 *     prices on screen beside it.
 *   - The sticky-bar condenser is still not ported and is still not wanted.
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Section from '../components/layout/Section.astro';
import Button from '../components/ui/Button.astro';
import TabSwitcher from '../components/ui/TabSwitcher.astro';
import { breadcrumbs, faqPage } from '../lib/schema';
import { SITE } from '../data/site';
import { PRICING_FAQS, PLANS, COMPARE_LEAD, COMPARE_MORE, CREDIT_PAGES } from '../data/pricing';

const title = 'Pricing | CrowAgent';
const description =
  'CrowMark pricing: Starter £49 a month, Pro £149, Portfolio quoted. Annual billing saves 10%, and the seats and AI credits included in every plan are published on the page.';

/* WIRE VALUES read by /contact to preselect a subject. Not copy; do not tidy.
   The plan CTAs carry their own, from data/pricing.ts, so only these two are
   needed here. */
const REQUEST_ACCESS = '/contact?enquiry=limited-access#contact-form';
const CONTACT_BUYER = '/contact?product=buyer-side#contact-form';

/*
 * STRUCTURED DATA. The legacy page publishes BreadcrumbList and ItemList, and
 * check-seo-parity.js fails the build on a lost type. The ItemList is rebuilt
 * from the same PLANS array that renders the cards, so a price cannot be
 * corrected in one place and left stale in the other — the drift that put a
 * £99 price on an OG card and kept it there for a year.
 *
 * Portfolio carries no Offer. It has no published price, and an Offer with no
 * price is worse than no Offer. No aggregateRating and no review: this site
 * does not publish ratings it cannot evidence.
 */
const offers = PLANS.filter((p) => p.monthly !== null).map((p) => ({
  '@type': 'Offer',
  name: p.name,
  price: String(p.monthly),
  priceCurrency: 'GBP',
  url: `${SITE.origin}/pricing`,
}));

const schema = [
  breadcrumbs([
    { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
    { name: 'Pricing', path: '/pricing' },
  ]),
  {
    '@type': 'ItemList',
    itemListElement: [
      {
        '@type': 'ListItem',
        position: 1,
        item: {
          '@type': 'SoftwareApplication',
          name: 'CrowMark',
          applicationCategory: 'BusinessApplication',
          operatingSystem: 'Web',
          url: `${SITE.origin}/crowmark`,
          publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'CrowAgent', url: `${SITE.origin}/` },
          offers,
        },
      },
    ],
  },
  ...faqPage(PRICING_FAQS),
];

const SUPPLIER_CAPSULES = [
  'Contracts Finder and Find a Tender',
  'PPN 002 · 10% minimum',
  'PPN 017 disclosure',
  'Procurement Act s.52 and s.71',
];

const BUYER_CAPSULES = [
  'Requirement builder',
  'Verbatim evidence locator',
  'Advisory only, never scores',
  'Delivery monitoring',
];

const TRUST = [
  { state: 'yes', label: 'UK-hosted data on Supabase and Vercel' },
  { state: 'yes', label: 'Volume licensing and invoice billing' },
  { state: 'yes', label: 'Single sign-on via SAML 2.0, with SCIM provisioning' },
  { state: 'soon', label: 'SOC 2 evidence pack (on the roadmap)' },
];
---

<Base title={title} description={description} path="/pricing" schema={schema}>
  <Section
    as="h1"
    eyebrow="Published pricing · No quote required"
    title="See the price first. Sized for SMEs."
    standfirst="Three plans from £49 a month. The seats each plan includes, and the AI credits it is metered on, are printed here rather than discovered after you pay."
    id="hero"
  >
    <div class="btn-row">
      <Button href={REQUEST_ACCESS}>Request access</Button>
      <Button href="#suppliers" variant="secondary">See the plans</Button>
    </div>
  </Section>

  {/*
    The whole priced area is one wrapper so the billing checkbox can sit ahead of
    every price it governs and reach them with a sibling combinator. The input is
    first in source order for exactly that reason.
  */}
  <div class="pricing">
    {/*
      `data-tab-for="suppliers"` ON BOTH HALVES OF THE SWITCH, O-57. The input
      is one element and its visible track is another, several nodes away, so
      both have to say which panel they belong to or half of the control goes
      and the other half stays keyboard-reachable. The contract is
      scripts/tabs.ts's; the reason the behaviour exists is recorded there.

      Only the supplier plans have two prices. Buyer pricing is "Contact sales"
      with a scoped annual contract behind it, so monthly against annual is not
      a choice a buyer has, and a switch that moves nothing invites a reader to
      press it and conclude the page is broken.

      THE SWITCH IS HIDDEN, NOT RESET. A reader who chose annual, looked at the
      buyer side and came back finds it where they left it: `hidden` does not
      clear `:checked`, so the two rules above still resolve and the annual
      prices are still the ones showing when the supplier panel returns.
      Measured on the round trip rather than assumed.
    */}
    <input
      type="checkbox"
      id="billing-toggle"
      class="billing__input"
      data-tab-for="suppliers"
      aria-label="Show annual prices, which are 10% lower"
    />

    {/*
      THE STICKY BAR, AND THE SWITCHER THAT NOW SWITCHES.

      This block used to carry a <ul class="tabs"> of two bare anchor links, and
      the comment above it said "both sections now always render, so the links
      simply work". They did work as links. They did not work as the control
      they were drawn to look like, and the owner said so on 2026-08-03: "why
      everything is visible by deafult if thats the case why we need the product
      switcher?" Measured on the running build before this change, both
      #suppliers (1089px) and #buyers (1279px) were display: block at all times,
      with no role="tablist" and no role="tabpanel" anywhere on the document.

      The behaviour and the appearance both moved into TabSwitcher.astro, which
      is the point of the change rather than a side effect of it. The legacy
      site solved this in js/modules/pricing-tabs-panel.js, a file that knew this
      page's id suffix and this page's table ids and was loaded by this page
      alone; the second tabbed surface would have been a second copy. Everything
      the component needs from here is two labels and two ids that this page
      already renders.

      SCOPE, MEASURED ON :8092 RATHER THAN INFERRED. Only the two audience
      sections are inside the reference's switchable panels. Its comparison
      table, AI credits and FAQ sit outside them and stay visible on both tabs.
      That is what this does too, so nothing on this page is hidden that the
      reference does not hide.

      The bar stays a <nav>. Without JavaScript these are exactly what the
      landmark says they are, two in-page links, and that is the state the
      server always sends; the tablist is layered on top of it.
    */}
    <nav class="bar" aria-label="Pricing sections">
      <TabSwitcher
        label="Choose your side of the procurement"
        tabs={[
          { id: 'suppliers', label: 'For suppliers' },
          { id: 'buyers', label: 'For buyers' },
        ]}
      />

      <div class="billing" data-tab-for="suppliers">
        <span class="billing__label billing__label--m">Monthly</span>
        <label for="billing-toggle" class="billing__hit">
          <span class="billing__track"><span class="billing__knob"></span></span>
        </label>
        <span class="billing__label billing__label--a">Annual · Save 10%</span>
      </div>
    </nav>

    <Section
      eyebrow="Bid and tender software"
      title="CrowMark for Suppliers"
      standfirst="Respond to tenders, RFPs, RFIs, PQQs and SQs, in the public sector and the private sector."
      id="suppliers"
    >
      <ul class="capsules" role="list">
        {SUPPLIER_CAPSULES.map((c) => <li>{c}</li>)}
      </ul>
      <p class="viewlink"><a href="/crowmark">View product</a></p>

      <p class="note">
        Every plan below covers public and private sector bidding. Tender discovery, PPN 002
        scoring, PPN 017 disclosure and Procurement Act evidencing exist only in public
        procurement, so those apply to public sector work.
      </p>

      <p class="eyebrow kicker">Same plans, either sector</p>

      <ul class="grid grid--3 card-sync" role="list">
        {
          PLANS.map((p) => (
            <li class:list={['card', 'surface', 'plan', { 'plan--rec': p.recommended }]}>
              {/* TEXT, not fill and position. The legacy card marked Pro with a
                  coloured corner flag reading "Most Popular"; that much was fine,
                  but the same treatment on /crowmark carried no text at all
                  (see the note in crowmark.astro). Stated here as words. */}
              {p.recommended && <p class="chip plan__flag">Recommended</p>}
              <h3 class="card__h">{p.name}</h3>
              <p class="meta plan__who">{p.who}</p>

              {p.monthly === null ? (
                <p class="plan__price">Contact sales</p>
              ) : (
                <>
                  <p class="plan__price plan__price--m">
                    £{p.monthly}
                    <span class="plan__per">/mo</span>
                  </p>
                  <p class="plan__price plan__price--a">
                    £{p.annual!.toLocaleString('en-GB')}
                    <span class="plan__per">/yr · 10% off</span>
                  </p>
                </>
              )}

              <p class="card__body">{p.body}</p>
              <p class="card__cta">
                <Button href={p.href} variant={p.recommended ? 'primary' : 'secondary'}>
                  {p.cta}<span class="visually-hidden"> for the {p.name} plan</span>
                </Button>
              </p>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </Section>

    <Section
      eyebrow="Bid and tender software"
      title="CrowMark for Buyers"
      standfirst="Publish requirements, then locate the evidence for each one across every response you receive."
      id="buyers"
    >
      <ul class="capsules" role="list">
        {BUYER_CAPSULES.map((c) => <li>{c}</li>)}
      </ul>
      <p class="viewlink"><a href="/crowmark-buyers">View product</a></p>

      {/*
        CORRECTED. This read "Everything else on this page, including the AI
        credit model, works the same way." It does not: credit-allowances.ts maps
        every council_* tier to null, which that file defines as fair use with
        deliberately no published number on any tier. The supplier credit table
        below does not describe the buyer side.
      */}
      <p class="note">
        Buyer engagements are scoped to the organisation rather than sold by seat, so there is
        no self-serve price. AI usage is fair use, scoped in the contract, rather than the
        published monthly credit allowance the supplier plans carry.
      </p>

      <p class="eyebrow kicker">Buyer pricing</p>

      <ul class="grid grid--buyer" role="list">
        <li class="card surface plan plan--wide">
          <h3 class="card__h">Buyer side</h3>
          <p class="meta plan__who">Contracting authorities and private sector procurement teams</p>
          <p class="plan__price">
            Contact sales<span class="plan__per">Scoped to your organisation</span>
          </p>
          <p class="card__body">
            Billed by invoice or purchase order on annual terms. Tiers scale with organisation
            size. If you need to buy through a framework, tell us which one and we will confirm
            what is possible.
          </p>
          <p class="eyebrow kicker kicker--tight">What it covers</p>
          <ul class="ticks" role="list">
            <li>Requirement builder with a deterministic social value rubric</li>
            <li>Evidence located per published requirement, quoted verbatim</li>
            <li>Evaluation workspace across directorates</li>
            <li>Delivery monitoring against what was promised</li>
            <li>Invoice or PO billing, SAML 2.0 single sign-on, DPA and security review</li>
          </ul>
          {/* `btn-row`, not a hand-rolled flex row. This was the FIFTEENTH private
              two-button container on the site — styles/button-row.css retired
              fourteen of them — and it behaved exactly as that file predicts: at
              390 it wrapped and left both buttons pinned to the card's left
              gutter under centred copy, which is what scripts/check-render.js
              caught. The shared row equalises the two widths, centres itself and
              goes full width on a phone. */}
          <p class="card__cta btn-row">
            <Button href={CONTACT_BUYER}>Book a demo</Button>
            <Button href="#faq" variant="secondary">Read the FAQ</Button>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li class="card surface plan">
          <h3 class="card__h">Why no published price?</h3>
          <p class="card__body">
            A buyer engagement is not a seat count. It is scoped to the organisation, procured
            under your own rules and billed the way your finance team can actually pay, so a
            single self-serve number would be misleading rather than transparent.
          </p>
          <p class="card__body">
            We publish supplier pricing precisely because that side can be published honestly.
            Where it cannot, we say so instead of guessing.
          </p>
          <p class="eyebrow kicker kicker--tight">Talk to us if</p>
          <ul class="ticks ticks--plain" role="list">
            <li>You run competitions and evaluate responses</li>
            <li>You need multi-entity or group consolidation</li>
            <li>You need invoice or PO billing, single sign-on or a DPA</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="card__cta">
            <Button href={CONTACT_BUYER} variant="secondary">Contact sales</Button>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </Section>

    <Section
      eyebrow="Feature depth"
      title="Compare the details."
      standfirst="Every plan runs the same statutory checks. What changes between them is seats, AI credits and post-award tracking."
      id="compare"
    >
      <h3 class="cmp__h"><span class="cmp__dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>CrowMark</h3>

      <div class="cmp__scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="CrowMark features by plan, scrollable">
        <table class="cmp">
          <caption class="visually-hidden">CrowMark features by plan</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Feature</th>
              <th scope="col">Starter</th>
              <th scope="col">Pro</th>
              <th scope="col">Portfolio</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              COMPARE_LEAD.map((r) => (
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">{r.feature}</th>
                  {r.cells.map((c) => (
                    <td>
                      {c === true ? (
                        <>
                          <span class="tick" aria-hidden="true">✓</span>
                          <span class="visually-hidden">Included</span>
                        </>
                      ) : c === false ? (
                        <>
                          <span class="dash" aria-hidden="true">–</span>
                          <span class="visually-hidden">Not included</span>
                        </>
                      ) : (
                        c
                      )}
                    </td>
                  ))}
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <details class="cmp__more">
          {/* `meta` MOVED FROM THE SPAN TO THE SUMMARY, 2026-08-04, A-48. It sat
              on `.cmp__show` only, while the summary restated the recipe at
              0.12em for itself - so the two spans inside one control tracked
              differently, 0.1em on the one with the class and 0.12em on the one
              without. Only ever one of them is visible, which is why nobody saw
              it. One label, one class, on the element that IS the label. */}
          <summary class="meta">
            <span class="cmp__show">Show {COMPARE_MORE.length} more features</span>
            <span class="cmp__hide">Show fewer features</span>
          </summary>
          <table class="cmp">
            <caption class="visually-hidden">Additional CrowMark features by plan</caption>
            <thead class="visually-hidden">
              <tr>
                <th scope="col">Feature</th>
                <th scope="col">Starter</th>
                <th scope="col">Pro</th>
                <th scope="col">Portfolio</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {
                COMPARE_MORE.map((r) => (
                  <tr>
                    <th scope="row">{r.feature}</th>
                    {r.cells.map((c) => (
                      <td>
                        {c === true ? (
                          <>
                            <span class="tick" aria-hidden="true">✓</span>
                            <span class="visually-hidden">Included</span>
                          </>
                        ) : (
                          <>
                            <span class="dash" aria-hidden="true">–</span>
                            <span class="visually-hidden">Not included</span>
                          </>
                        )}
                      </td>
                    ))}
                  </tr>
                ))
              }
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </details>
      </div>

      {/*
        THE OA-05 ANSWER, SAID OUT LOUD RATHER THAN IMPLIED. "Unlimited" on the
        bids row is true and the credits row is the thing that is capped, so the
        page says which is which instead of leaving a reader to reconcile them.
      */}
      <p class="note">
        Bids are not metered, which is why that row reads unlimited on every plan. The meter is
        AI credits, and the number each plan includes is the row below it.
      </p>

      <p class="viewlink"><a href="#faq">See all pricing FAQs</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section
      eyebrow="AI credits"
      title="How AI usage is counted."
      standfirst="One credit is one AI generation, such as a drafted tender answer or a delivery evidence summary. Everything we calculate rather than generate, including PPN 002 social value arithmetic, scoring and exports, is never charged and never capped."
      id="ai-credits"
    >
      <div class="stack">
        {/*
          PUBLISHED FOR THE FIRST TIME. The legacy page explained the credit
          model in two long cards and never once said how many credits a plan
          includes, which is the only question the model raises. Numbers from
          credit-allowances.ts CREDIT_ALLOWANCES.crowmark.
        */}
        <div class="card surface">
          <h3 class="card__h">What each plan includes</h3>
          <div class="cmp__scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="AI credits per plan, scrollable">
            <table class="cmp cmp--slim">
              <caption class="visually-hidden">AI credits included per plan, per calendar month</caption>
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="col">Plan</th>
                  <th scope="col">AI credits a month</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {
                  PLANS.map((p) => (
                    <tr>
                      <th scope="row">{p.name}</th>
                      <td>{p.credits.toLocaleString('en-GB')}</td>
                    </tr>
                  ))
                }
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p class="card__body">
            These are the numbers the platform enforces. Bids, contracts, seats within your
            plan, exports and every deterministic calculation are not metered against them.
            Re-drafting the same answer is free for the first three re-drafts each month; the
            fourth costs one credit. A generation that fails is not charged.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="card surface">
          <h3 class="card__h">Reading a tender document is charged by page</h3>
          <p class="card__body">
            Uploading a tender document and extracting its requirements costs
            <strong> 3 credits per 10 pages, rounded up</strong>. A 10-page invitation to tender
            costs 3 credits. Here is what counts as a page:
          </p>
