{/*
  THE ONLY GLOBAL RULES ON THIS PAGE, AND WHY THEY HAVE TO BE.

  The checkbox lives in pricing.astro's template, and so do the two price
  paragraphs — but the <section> BETWEEN them is rendered by Section.astro and
  carries Section's scope hash, not this page's. A fully scoped sibling selector
  would have its intermediate step rewritten with this component's hash and would
  never match. `:global()` is applied to that one intermediate step only, so both
  ends of the selector stay scoped.

  These cannot leak: every rule is anchored on `#billing-toggle`, an id that
  exists on this route and nowhere else in the build.
*/}
<style is:global>
  #billing-toggle:not(:checked) ~ * .plan__price--a { display: none; }
  #billing-toggle:checked ~ * .plan__price--m { display: none; }
</style>

<style>
  .row--left { justify-content: flex-start; margin: var(--stack-2) 0 0; }

  .kicker {
    margin: var(--sec-gap) 0 var(--stack-1);
    text-align: center;
    color: var(--c-text-muted);
  }
  /* CENTRED SINCE 2026-08-03. This carried `text-align: left` and an entry in
     check-design-system.js arguing that a label over a list should share the
     list's left edge. The owner overruled it: "all the text in cards or non
     cards must be in central". The list beneath still reads down its own edge,
     because a list item does, and that is now stated once in
     styles/alignment.css rather than page by page. */
  .kicker--tight { margin: var(--stack-2) 0 var(--stack-1); }

  .note {
    margin: var(--stack-2) auto 0;
    max-width: 72ch;
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-body);
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--c-text-sub);
  }

  .viewlink {
    margin: var(--stack-2) 0 0;
    text-align: center;
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-body);
  }
  /* WCAG 2.2 target size. These sit alone on their own line rather than inline
     in a sentence, so the 2.5.8 inline exception does not apply to them; they
     measured 22px tall before this rule. */
  .viewlink a,
  .note a {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    min-height: 44px;
    color: var(--c-interactive);
  }
  .viewlink a:focus-visible,
  .note a:focus-visible {
    outline: 3px solid var(--c-interactive);
    outline-offset: 3px;
  }

  /* ── The sticky bar ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Same job as the legacy `.pricing-sticky`: the audience switch and the
     billing switch on one line under the nav. Opaque, because the legacy one
     was translucent and pricing text bled through it on scroll (P1-002). */
  .bar {
    position: sticky;
    /*
     * NOT `top: 0`. Nav.astro is itself `position: sticky; top: 0; z-index: 100`,
     * so a second sticky at 0 does not stack below it — it parks in the same
     * band and the nav paints over it. Measured before and after: the tab row
     * and the billing switch were half-hidden behind the nav at 1440, 768 and
     * 390. The nav is 73px at all three widths (its row height is fixed, not
     * fluid), and there is no token for it, because this is the first component
     * outside the nav that needs to know. Declared once here rather than typed
     * into three rules. crowmark.astro's `.subnav` has the same `top: 0` and
     * therefore the same defect; it is outside this task's scope and reported
     * rather than changed.
     */
    --bar-top: 73px;
    top: var(--bar-top);
    z-index: 20;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: var(--stack-1) clamp(16px, 4vw, 40px);
    padding: 0 var(--gutter);
    border-block: 1px solid var(--c-border);
    background: var(--c-bg);
  }

  /* The switcher's geometry, states, focus ring and reduced-motion handling all
     live in components/ui/TabSwitcher.astro. This page used to hold 24 lines
     describing two outlined pills, which is exactly the per-page treatment that
     let the control drift away from the one it was drawn from. Nothing about it
     is restated here. */

  /* ── The billing switch ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
     A real checkbox, clipped rather than display:none so it stays focusable and
     stays in the accessibility tree. The visible track is a <label for>, sized
     to a 44px hit area with the 26px track centred inside it. */
  .billing__input {
    position: absolute;
    width: 1px;
    height: 1px;
    margin: -1px;
    padding: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
    clip-path: inset(50%);
    white-space: nowrap;
    border: 0;
  }

  .billing { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 10px; }

  /* O-57. scripts/tabs.ts sets `hidden` on this row while the buyer panel is up,
     which is what takes it out of the tab order and out of the accessibility
     tree. It is not what PAINTS it: the `display: flex` above is an author rule
     and the user-agent `[hidden]` rule loses to it.
     O-59: the local `.billing[hidden] { display: none }` that used to resolve
     that conflict here is gone. tokens.css now carries the rule once, unlayered
     and !important, so the row that declares a display no longer has to also
     remember to undeclare it. This page was one of the five restatements. */

  .billing__label {
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
    font-weight: 600;
    white-space: nowrap;
    color: var(--c-text-muted);
    transition: color var(--dur) var(--ease);
  }
  #billing-toggle:not(:checked) ~ .bar .billing__label--m,
  #billing-toggle:checked ~ .bar .billing__label--a { color: var(--c-text); }

  .billing__hit {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    min-width: 44px;
    min-height: 44px;
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  .billing__track {
    position: relative;
    display: block;
    width: 50px;
    height: 26px;
    border: 1px solid var(--c-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-pill);
    background: var(--c-panel);
    transition: background-color var(--dur) var(--ease), border-color var(--dur) var(--ease);
  }
  .billing__knob {
    position: absolute;
    top: 3px;
    left: 3px;
    width: 18px;
    height: 18px;
    border-radius: var(--radius-pill);
    background: var(--c-text);
    transition: transform var(--dur) var(--ease);
  }
  #billing-toggle:checked ~ .bar .billing__track {
    background: var(--c-interactive);
    border-color: var(--c-interactive);
  }
  #billing-toggle:checked ~ .bar .billing__knob { transform: translateX(24px); }
  #billing-toggle:focus-visible ~ .bar .billing__track {
    outline: 3px solid var(--c-interactive);
    outline-offset: 3px;
  }

  /* ── Capsules ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .capsules {
    list-style: none;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: 8px;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
  }
  .capsules li {
    padding: 0.4rem 0.8rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--c-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-pill);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    line-height: var(--lh-tight);
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
    color: var(--c-text-muted);
  }

  /* ── Cards. Geometry only: the treatment lives in styles/surfaces.css. ─── */
  .grid { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; display: grid; gap: clamp(18px, 2.2vw, 30px); }
  .grid--3 { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
  .grid--buyer { grid-template-columns: 2fr 1fr; align-items: start; }

  /* How many parts each `card-sync` row's cards are built from. A fact about
     the markup, not a spacing decision; see styles/card-row.css. Keyed to the
     section because the same `.grid--3` recipe carries a six-part plan card
     under #suppliers and a two-part answer card under #faq. */
  #suppliers .grid { --card-parts: 6; }
  #faq .grid { --card-parts: 2; }

  /* `minmax(0, 1fr)`, never a bare `1fr`. A grid item's default `min-width` is
     `auto`, which is min-CONTENT, and these cards hold tables with a floor
     width. Left as `1fr` the card refuses to shrink below its table and pushes
     the page sideways at 390px: measured 536px of card inside a 390px viewport,
     with the horizontal scrollbar on <html> rather than on the table wrapper
     that exists to carry it. Every grid on this page is stated this way. */
  .stack { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); gap: clamp(18px, 2.2vw, 30px); }
  .solo { max-width: var(--measure-band); margin-inline: auto; }

  /* Geometry only, plus the same shrink permission the grids above need: a flex
     or grid child holding a min-width table has to be allowed below min-content
     or the wrapper's overflow never engages. */
  .card { padding: var(--blk-pad); min-width: 0; }

  .card__h {
    margin: 0 0 var(--stack-1);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--t-h4);
    font-weight: 700;
    line-height: 1.3;
    color: var(--c-text);
  }

  .card__body {
    margin: 0 0 var(--stack-1);
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-body);
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--c-text-sub);
  }
  .card__body:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }

  /* `margin-block`, NOT the `margin` shorthand. styles/button-row.css warns in
     as many words that a shorthand written after it silently resets its
     `margin-inline: auto`, and that is exactly what happened here the moment
     this element took `btn-row`: the row measured 147px left of the card's
     centre. Longhand, so the row keeps its own centring and every other
     .card__cta keeps the zero inline margin a <p> has anyway. */
  .card__cta { margin-block: var(--stack-2) 0; }

  /* ── Plans ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  /* ── NO COLOURED BORDER, WHICH IS WHAT PRODUCTION DOES ────────────────────
   *
   * Owner, 2026-08-03: "I don't want card border with colour, follow exactly the
   * same as production... this is for the card border on the pricing page only."
   *
   * Measured on the deployed /pricing rather than assumed. All three plan cards
   * there carry a WHITE border and nothing else:
   *
   *   Starter    1px rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.12)
   *   Popular    1px rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.08), plus a white inset highlight
   *   Portfolio  1px rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.12)
   *
   * This build had a 3px violet rule down the left of every plan and a teal
   * ring on the recommended one. Both are gone. The recommended plan is still
   * marked, by the RECOMMENDED flag it already carries, which is how production
   * marks it too.
   *
   * The border now comes from `.surface`, so these cards look like every other
   * card on the site and change in one place. */
  /* ── O-44 · THE BADGE KEEPS ITS ROW ON EVERY CARD ────────────────────────
   *
   * THE DEFECT, MEASURED ON THE BUILT PAGE AT 1440 IN BOTH BILLING MODES.
   * Only Pro carries a "Recommended" chip, and under `display: flex` the other
   * two cards simply started one element higher: three plan names at 1398.3 and
   * 1438.7, three sub-labels 40.4px apart, three prices 40.4px apart. The
   * buttons were already level at 1793.7 — `margin-top: auto` below sees to
   * that — so the row was pinned at the bottom and ragged at the top, which is
   * the half a reader compares first.
   *
   * TAKEN OUT OF FLOW, OR RESERVED? RESERVED, AND THE ROW RESERVES IT.
   * Absolute positioning would have needed the badge's height written down as a
   * number in this file, and then a second number for the gap under it, both of
   * which move the day the chip's type or padding changes. The row is a
   * `card-sync`, so the six parts are the container's own tracks (see
   * styles/card-row.css); naming the line each part sits on means Starter and
   * Portfolio leave track one EMPTY, and the track is still sized by the badge
   * on the card that has one. The height is reserved because a sibling needs
   * it, not because somebody measured it.
   *
   * Both price paragraphs take the same line. Exactly one of them is displayed
   * at a time — the billing checkbox hides the other — and a `display: none`
   * grid item occupies no track, so the row is the visible price's whatever the
   * mode.
   *
   * `display: flex; flex-direction: column` USED TO BE HERE and had to go: a
   * scoped rule is unlayered, so it beat the shared recipe and the card would
   * have kept its private column while the container handed out tracks. */
  .plan__flag { grid-row: 1; }
  .plan .card__h { grid-row: 2; }
  .plan__who { grid-row: 3; }
  .plan__price { grid-row: 4; }
  .plan .card__body { grid-row: 5; }
  .plan .card__cta { grid-row: 6; }

  .plan--wide { grid-column: auto; }

  .plan__flag {
    /* `justify-self`, NOT `align-self`, and the difference is not cosmetic.
       In the flex column this replaced, `align-self` was the cross axis and
       therefore the horizontal one; in a grid it is the block axis, so the same
       declaration would have centred the chip VERTICALLY in its track and left
       it stretched to the full width of the card. */
    justify-self: center;
    margin: 0 0 var(--stack-1);
    /* O-35, owner decision 2026-08-04: MOVE THE BADGE, NOT THE REFUSAL HUE.
       This was a FILLED --c-ambient-end chip. V6 moved refused from #C77DFF to
       #B39DFB, four degrees from the ambient violet, so a filled violet
       RECOMMENDED sat in the same hue family as REFUSED — on a site whose whole
       argument is that violet means the engine refused something. That is a
       credibility hazard rather than a cosmetic one.
       Refusal keeps violet because it is the load-bearing semantic and
       everything else yields to it. So this becomes an OUTLINE chip: no fill,
       accent text and border. It reads as emphasis without claiming a hue.
       --c-interactive is the right token and not merely an available one: this
       is emphasis on a card, not a mark, and a mark may not appear where no
       evidence is being asserted. It is the same token the selected tab pill
       takes, so the two loudest non-semantic emphases on the site now agree.
       Directive W7: one semantic per hue, and violet is spoken for. */
    background: none;
    color: var(--c-interactive);
    border-color: var(--c-interactive);
  }

  /* Margin and colour only. `line-height: 1.5` was here until 2026-08-04 and
     was one of three components each answering "what leading does a label that
     wraps take" with its own number; styles/labels.css now answers it once, for
     the whole family, and the argument is that all-caps has no descenders.
     Deleting this moved these four lines by 3.25px. */
  .plan__who {
    margin: 0 0 var(--stack-2);
    color: var(--c-text-muted);
  }

  .plan__price {
    margin: 0 0 var(--stack-2);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--t-h3);
    font-weight: 800;
    line-height: 1.15;
    color: var(--c-text);
  }
  .plan__per {
    display: block;
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-body);
    font-weight: 400;
    color: var(--c-text-muted);
  }

  /* `margin-top: auto` LEFT WITH THE FLEX COLUMN ABOVE. It was what levelled
     the three buttons, and the shared track now does that: the action is line 6
     on every card, so the three sit on one line because they are the same row
     rather than because each was pushed to its own floor. */
  .plan .card__cta { padding-top: var(--stack-2); }
  /* `.plan__cta-row` is DELETED, not restyled: it was a private copy of
     .btn-row, and the markup now uses the shared one. */

  .ticks {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 8px;
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-body);
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--c-text-sub);
  }
  .ticks li { padding-left: 1.4em; text-indent: -1.4em; }
  .ticks li::before { content: '◔ '; color: var(--c-ambient-end); }
  .ticks--plain li::before { content: '• '; color: var(--c-ambient-start); }

  /* ── Comparison table ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .cmp__h {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 12px;
    margin: 0 0 var(--stack-2);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--t-h4);
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--c-text);
  }
  .cmp__dot {
    width: 8px;
    height: 8px;
    border-radius: var(--radius-pill);
    /* O-35 / W7. A bullet marking a comparison row is not a refusal. Moved out
       of the violet family with the RECOMMENDED badge, .card--here and
       .step__lbl — the four that O-35 named. */
    background: var(--c-interactive);
  }

  /* The table has a floor width so its columns stay readable; the wrapper is
     what scrolls, so the page itself never does.
     `position: relative` IS LOAD-BEARING AND WAS NOT OBVIOUS. Every tick and
     dash cell carries a `.visually-hidden` span reading "Included" or "Not
     included", and that class is `position: absolute`. An absolutely positioned
     box is clipped by an ancestor's overflow only when that ancestor is its
     containing block, and without this line the nearest positioned ancestor was
     `.section` — so ~30 one-pixel spans took their static positions from cells
     up to 780px out, escaped the scroll box entirely, and dragged the document
     to 686px at a 390px viewport. Measured: the page scrolled sideways 296px
     while every visible element was correctly clipped, and `overflow: hidden` on
     this wrapper did not fix it, because the problem was never the overflow
     value. Making the wrapper the containing block puts them back inside it. */
  .cmp__scroll { position: relative; overflow-x: auto; -webkit-overflow-scrolling: touch; }
  /* It carries tabindex="0" so a keyboard user can scroll it (axe
     scrollable-region-focusable, raised at 390px). Anything focusable needs a
     visible ring, or the tabindex has moved focus somewhere invisible. */
  .cmp__scroll:focus-visible { outline: 3px solid var(--c-interactive); outline-offset: 3px; }

  /*
   * `table-layout: fixed`, and a floor width low enough that the first plan
   * column is partly on screen at 390px. With auto layout the feature column
   * claimed 446 of 760px, so at 390 the reader saw a column headed "Feature"
   * and three plan columns entirely off-screen with nothing to suggest they
   * existed — a scrollable table that does not look scrollable is a table with
   * missing data. Fixed layout wraps the long feature labels instead of letting
   * them set the width, and 620px leaves Starter half-visible, which is the
   * affordance.
   */
  .cmp {
    width: 100%;
    min-width: 620px;
    table-layout: fixed;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-body);
    text-align: left;
  }
  .cmp th:first-child { width: 40%; }
  .cmp--slim { min-width: 420px; }
  .cmp--slim th:first-child { width: 34%; }
  .cmp th,
  .cmp td {
    /* Padding and alignment are styles/blocks.css — one cell recipe sitewide. */
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--c-border);
    color: var(--c-text-sub);
  }
  /* THE RECIPE IS GONE FROM HERE, 2026-08-04, A-48. It was a hand-copy of
     styles/prose.css's column header, made because `.prose th` is a treatment
